#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717110 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{1}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{1}{2}\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n2x&=\\cos\\theta \\\\ \ny&=\\frac{1}{2}\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n2y&=\\sin\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^2&=\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n4y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $\\frac{1}{4}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^2+4y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n4\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $\\frac{1}{2}$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717118 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{3}{2}x&=\\cos\\theta \\\\ \ny&=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{3}{2}y&=\\sin\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{9}{4}x^2&=\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{9}{4}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $\\frac{4}{9}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{9}{4}x^2+\\frac{9}{4}y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{9}{4}\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=\\frac{4}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{4}{9}$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $\\frac{2}{3}$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717139 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2-3\\sin x}{4}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the function to identify the coefficient of sine:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2-3\\sin x}{4}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\sin x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=-\\frac{3}{4}$, so the amplitude is $\\left|-\\frac{3}{4}\\right|=\\frac{3}{4}$. The vertical shift $\\frac{1}{2}$ does not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717147 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2\\left(\\frac{1-3\\cos\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}{4}\\right)+1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, distribute the outer factor and collect the constant terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=\\frac{-2+6\\cos\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}{4}+1\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\\cos\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}+1\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\\cos\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven a function in the form $f(x)=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nThe coefficient of cosine is $A=\\frac{(-2)(-3)}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}$, so the amplitude is $\\left|\\frac{3}{2}\\right|=\\frac{3}{2}$. Both vertical scale factors contribute; the constant terms and the transformations inside the cosine do not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717152 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=3\\cos(2x+1)$?" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=3$, so the amplitude is $|3|=3$. The constants inside the cosine affect horizontal transformations, not the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717162 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{7}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nDividing each equation by $\\frac{7}{2}$ and squaring gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{49}x^2=\\sin^2\\theta,\\qquad \\frac{4}{49}y^2=\\cos^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ to eliminate $\\theta$. Choose the Cartesian equation." :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{7}{2}\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{7}x&=\\sin\\theta \\\\ \ny&=\\frac{7}{2}\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{7}y&=\\cos\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{49}x^2&=\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{49}y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $\\frac{49}{4}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{49}x^2+\\frac{4}{49}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{49}\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=\\frac{49}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{49}{4}$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $\\frac{7}{2}$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717166 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{9}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{9}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nDividing each equation by $\\frac{9}{2}$ and squaring gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{81}x^2=\\cos^2\\theta,\\qquad \\frac{4}{81}y^2=\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ to eliminate $\\theta$. Choose the Cartesian equation." :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{9}{2}\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{9}x&=\\cos\\theta \\\\ \ny&=\\frac{9}{2}\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{9}y&=\\sin\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{81}x^2&=\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{81}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $\\frac{81}{4}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{81}x^2+\\frac{4}{81}y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{81}\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=\\frac{81}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{81}{4}$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $\\frac{9}{2}$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717167 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nDividing each equation by $2$ and squaring gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}x^2=\\sin^2\\theta,\\qquad \\frac{1}{4}y^2=\\cos^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ to eliminate $\\theta$. Choose the Cartesian equation." :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{1}{2}x&=\\sin\\theta \\\\ \ny&=2\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{1}{2}y&=\\cos\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4}x^2&=\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{1}{4}y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4}x^2+\\frac{1}{4}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{1}{4}\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=4$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $2$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717175 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nDividing each equation by $\\frac{11}{2}$ and squaring gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{121}x^2=\\sin^2\\theta,\\qquad \\frac{4}{121}y^2=\\cos^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ to eliminate $\\theta$. Choose the Cartesian equation." :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{11}x&=\\sin\\theta \\\\ \ny&=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{11}y&=\\cos\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{121}x^2&=\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{121}y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $\\frac{121}{4}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{121}x^2+\\frac{4}{121}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{121}\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=\\frac{121}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{121}{4}$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $\\frac{11}{2}$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717194 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e14068c370ca692ffcaa52e375dc5fd3892108df5db98a95804ae8d63b9eb2.png)\n\nThe solid arc is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$? Use $0\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$ and include both filled endpoints.\n\n$\\theta\\in[$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $]$" :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $3$ and the center is $(0,0)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=\\pi$ and ends at $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/94/94853c2bca1ac50aaa4d6fe59d44c66b3a1da1909430b77d322ab34d3e747189.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta,\\qquad\\theta\\in[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717202 :question/problem "![](images/e7/e7de690a6e5f0e1d6f55ab49f52cabc7ce2458552edfb7f4be1be222c55da4ad.png)\n\nThe solid arc is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$? Use $0\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$ and include both filled endpoints.\n\n$\\theta\\in[$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $]$" :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $4$ and the center is $(0,0)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/5a/5a9d5a4ddc8a990e5c3f6354b3651de6830232f4f7e3eded8ccf9d183ad5af0c.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=4\\sin\\theta,\\qquad\\theta\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717204 :question/problem "![](images/31/31e8e80eba576b30284cca8296c2fe9537eba4415401d0c748533f9c7efc236d.png)\n\nThe solid arc is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$? Use $0\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$ and include both filled endpoints.\n\n$\\theta\\in[$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $]$" :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $6$ and the center is $(0,0)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=\\pi$ and ends at $\\theta=2\\pi$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/5e/5e36d3d207dbf8ad8b175c1c05c094cac96aa79fcb2bdd4e903a7442a2ead91c.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[\\pi,2\\pi]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad\\theta\\in[\\pi,2\\pi].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717209 :question/problem "![](images/76/76dc2eea299993b313c6ab2d629a49724f3e058d6cbdcb5e24e77c7ea1e8d007.png)\n\nThe solid arc is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$? Use $0\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$ and include both filled endpoints.\n\n$\\theta\\in[$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $]$" :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $5$ and the center is $(0,0)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $\\theta=\\pi$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/92/9261704bded632e6a3ae8bec6bb6ae608c71596c213280ea28860707c30c0285.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta,\\qquad\\theta\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717211 :question/problem "![](images/a9/a964dd6d3033b513ab6d2e55d86453648dab39507fd49d477550ff9466443e55.png)\n\nThe solid arc is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$? Use $0\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$ and include both filled endpoints.\n\n$\\theta\\in[$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $]$" :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $1$ and the center is $(0,0)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $\\theta=2\\pi$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/6e/6e1d2c53ac21f3163f680337ff2753d7a0db9d38d57dedd0788d206f9f4f5772.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[\\frac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta,\\qquad\\theta\\in[\\frac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717216 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\cos\\left(5x+\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function of the form $f(x)=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D$, the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi}{|B|}.\n$$\n\nHere the coefficient of $x$ inside the cosine is $B=5$. The outside factor $-3$ and the shifts do not change the period.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717218 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi-2x}{3}+\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Combine the $x$ terms inside the sine before applying the period formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\pi-2x}{3}+\\frac{x}{2}\n&=\\frac{\\pi}{3}-\\frac{2x}{3}+\\frac{x}{2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi}{3}+\\left(-\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{3}{6}\\right)x\\\\\n&=-\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{\\pi}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $B=-\\frac{1}{6}$. A negative input coefficient reverses the direction of a cycle; the period is a positive length, so use $|B|$:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi}{|B|}=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\frac{1}{6}}=12\\pi.\n$$\n\nUsing only one of the two original $x$ terms would miss their combined effect. The outside factor $3$ and vertical shift $-4$ do not affect the period."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717231 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2\\cos\\left(\\frac{3x-60^{\\circ}}{2}\\right)+4.\n$$\n\nAngles are measured in degrees." :question/worked-solution "First rewrite the entire cosine argument to identify the coefficient of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x-60^{\\circ}}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}x-30^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThus $B=\\frac{3}{2}$, rather than $3$. In degree measure the period is $T=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{|B|}$, so\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{\\frac{3}{2}}=360^{\\circ}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}=240^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThe factor $-2$ outside the cosine and the horizontal and vertical shifts do not change the period."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717235 :question/problem "![](images/b8/b808ca263a43775db59b313adbfa7fe0a408d3ce2c9427ebfe509403edf29c8c.png)\n\nThe solid arc is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta+4,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta+5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$? Use $0\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$ and include both filled endpoints." :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $1$ and the center is $(4,5)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=\\pi$ and ends at $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/92/9214153996ceb503d4ae6db6efcecabc0b241c13090080fe18f25878cad96216.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta+4,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta+5,\\qquad\\theta\\in[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717239 :question/problem "![](images/f9/f90ce7ec0707632cfd8f0efffabfe4e9b8803f6ba6906b68b4f88117bb8e2339.png)\n\nThe solid arc is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta-3,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta-1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$? Use $0\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$ and include both filled endpoints." :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $5$ and the center is $(-3,-1)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=0$ and ends at $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/ab/ab123b01039725c01cc87fc8ac0100066f1c5dca61c47815c1436c3cf9315b64.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[0,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta-3,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta-1,\\qquad\\theta\\in[0,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717253 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-4$ and a maximum value of $8$." :question/worked-solution "The midline is the horizontal line halfway between the minimum and maximum values. Its height is their average:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{y_{\\text{min}}+y_{\\text{max}}}{2} \\\\\n  &= \\frac{-4+8}{2} \\\\\n  &= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n  &= 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=2$. The quantity $\\frac{8-(-4)}{2}=6$ is the amplitude, not the height of the midline."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717256 :question/problem "The graph below shows a transformed cosine function. Find the equation of its midline.\n\n![Graph of a sinusoidal function on coordinate axes.](images/d3/d33b35c70cf649d4281e2daa297da3c2918da553e2f93b80e484f357f94cb124.png)" :question/worked-solution "Read the vertical values at a crest and a trough: the maximum is $3$ and the minimum is $-5$. The horizontal coordinates of these points are not needed.\n\nThe midline lies halfway between these two heights:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{y_{\\text{min}}+y_{\\text{max}}}{2} \\\\\n  &= \\frac{-5+3}{2} \\\\\n  &= -1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=-1$. It is $4$ units below each crest and $4$ units above each trough. The $x$-axis is not the midline of this graph."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717265 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=3-2\\left[\\cos\\left(3x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)+4\\right].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The expression is not yet in the form $A\\cos(Bx+C)+D$. Distribute the factor $-2$ to both terms inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3-2\\cos\\left(3x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-8 \\\\\n     &= -2\\cos\\left(3x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the vertical shift is $D=-5$, so the midline is $y=-5$. The $-\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ inside the cosine affects horizontal position, not the height of the midline.\n\nTo check using extrema, the cosine term ranges from $-1$ to $1$, so the function ranges from $-7$ to $-3$. Their average is\n\n$$\n\\frac{-7+(-3)}{2}=-5,\n$$\n\nwhich gives the same midline."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717270 :question/problem "Let $b=\\langle -2,-4,3\\rangle$ and $a=\\langle 2,1,1\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$." :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(-2)+(1)(-4)+(1)(3)}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-4+3}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717277 :question/problem "Let $b=\\langle -1,-2,-2\\rangle$ and $a=\\langle -1,1,-2\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$." :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(-1)+(1)(-2)+(-2)(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2+4}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717286 :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle 0,-2\\sqrt{5},0\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle 1,-2\\sqrt{5},-3\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{b}a=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)(1)+(-2\\sqrt{5})(-2\\sqrt{5})+(0)(-3)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+20+0}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{30}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717289 :question/problem "Let $b=\\langle 2,2\\sqrt{2},1\\rangle$ and $a=\\langle 1,\\sqrt{2},-3\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$." :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(2)+(\\sqrt{2})(2\\sqrt{2})+(-3)(1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+4-3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717293 :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle -3,2\\sqrt{5},1\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle -3,-\\sqrt{5},1\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{b}a=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)(-3)+(2\\sqrt{5})(-\\sqrt{5})+(1)(1)}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-10+1}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717340 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{m}|=12$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{m}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ is $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{m}$ onto $\\mathbf{n}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\mathbf{m}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{m}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{m}|=12$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\mathbf{m} &=|\\mathbf{m}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=12\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=12\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is positive because the included angle is acute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717346 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{d}|=12$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{d}$ onto $\\mathbf{c}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\mathbf{d}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{d}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{d}|=12$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\mathbf{d} &=|\\mathbf{d}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=12\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&=12\\cdot\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=- 6 \\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is negative because the included angle is obtuse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717350 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{c}|=12 \\sqrt{3}$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta=30^\\circ$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{c}$ onto $\\mathbf{d}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\mathbf{c}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{c}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{c}|=12 \\sqrt{3}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\mathbf{c} &=|\\mathbf{c}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=12 \\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(30^\\circ\\right) \\\\\n&=12 \\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=18.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is positive because the included angle is acute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717354 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{v}|=7 \\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{v}$ onto $\\mathbf{u}$." :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{v}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{v}|=7 \\sqrt{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{u}}\\mathbf{v} &=|\\mathbf{v}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=7 \\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&=7 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is positive because the included angle is acute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717355 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{m}|=6$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{m}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ is $\\theta=120^\\circ$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{m}$ onto $\\mathbf{n}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\mathbf{m}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the cosine of the included angle to find the signed scalar projection.\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{m}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{m}|=6$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\mathbf{m} &=|\\mathbf{m}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=6\\cos\\left(120^\\circ\\right) \\\\\n&=6\\cdot\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is negative because the included angle is obtuse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717389 :question/problem "Find the range of $f(x)=2\\sin\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+1$ for all real $x$." :question/worked-solution "The sine function takes every value from $-1$ to $1$, including both endpoints. Horizontal shifts and stretches do not change this range when $x$ can be any real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiply by $2$, then add $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2&\\le 2\\sin\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\le 2 \\\\\n-1&\\le 2\\sin\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+1\\le 3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth endpoints are attained, and every value between them is attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\\in[-1,3]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717394 :question/problem "Find the range of $f(x)=-3\\cos\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-1$ for all real $x$." :question/worked-solution "Horizontal shifts and stretches do not change the range of cosine over all real inputs, so\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-3$ reverses both inequality signs. Then subtract $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3&\\ge -3\\cos\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\ge -3 \\\\\n2&\\ge -3\\cos\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-1\\ge -4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the smaller endpoint first gives $-4\\le f(x)\\le 2$. Cosine attains both $1$ and $-1$ and all values between them, so the range is $f(x)\\in[-4,2]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717399 :question/problem "Find the range of $f(x)=1-2\\left(2-\\cos\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)$ for all real $x$." :question/worked-solution "First simplify the vertical transformations. The factor $-2$ multiplies both terms inside the outer parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=1-4+2\\cos\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&=2\\cos\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe effective vertical shift is $-3$, and the amplitude is $2$. Horizontal shifts and stretches do not change the range over all real inputs. Starting from the cosine bounds, multiply by $2$ and subtract $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1&\\le \\cos\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\le 1 \\\\\n-2&\\le 2\\cos\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\le 2 \\\\\n-5&\\le 2\\cos\\left(3x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-3\\le -1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCosine attains every value in $[-1,1]$, so every value between these transformed endpoints, including the endpoints, is attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\\in[-5,-1]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717533 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{-6\\sin\\left(x\\right)-2}{5}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the function to identify the whole coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)&=\\frac{-6\\sin\\left(x\\right)-2}{5} \\\\ &=-\\frac{6}{5}\\sin\\left(x\\right)-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=-\\frac{6}{5}$, so the amplitude is $\\left|-\\frac{6}{5}\\right|=\\frac{6}{5}$. Horizontal transformations and additive constants do not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717538 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{-6\\sin\\left(x\\right)+2}{6}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the function to identify the whole coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)&=\\frac{-6\\sin\\left(x\\right)+2}{6} \\\\ &=-\\sin\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=-1$, so the amplitude is $\\left|-1\\right|=1$. Horizontal transformations and additive constants do not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717540 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{-3\\sin\\left(x\\right)-2}{3}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the function to identify the whole coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)&=\\frac{-3\\sin\\left(x\\right)-2}{3} \\\\ &=-\\sin\\left(x\\right)-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=-1$, so the amplitude is $\\left|-1\\right|=1$. Horizontal transformations and additive constants do not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717541 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{7}{6}\\cos\\left(\\frac{1}{3}x+3\\right)+2$?" :question/worked-solution "For a function in the form $f(x)=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=-\\frac{7}{6}$, so the amplitude is $\\left|-\\frac{7}{6}\\right|=\\frac{7}{6}$. Horizontal transformations and additive constants do not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717548 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sin\\left(3x-1\\right)-2$?" :question/worked-solution "For a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so the amplitude is $\\left|- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right|=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Horizontal transformations and additive constants do not change the amplitude."}]}
